{% comment %}
  Joy Wholefoods "Build Your Subscription" landing — bespoke section for ONE shop
  (dantt-subscription-box.myshopify.com / joywholefoods.com.au theme).

  See docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-19-joyxjoy-landing-design.md §4 for the design
  this section implements. Do NOT copy this section into another theme/app — it is
  not a general Joy Subscription feature, it is custom work for one merchant.

  This section does exactly 3 things (spec §4):
    1. Inject `window.AVADA_JW = {bundles, categories}` from Liquid — 0 round-trip.
    2. Render the `.jw-landing-root` mount point the Preact bundle attaches to
       (packages/scripttag/src/subscriptionBoxJoyxjoy/managers/DisplayManager.js
       reads `window.AVADA_JW` and mounts `<LandingApp bundles categories />` into
       `document.querySelector('.jw-landing-root')`).
    3. Load the bundle script (`avada-subscription-box-joyxjoy-main.min.js`), URL
       configurable via a section setting — see "Bundle script URL" below for why.

  Everything else — hero copy, "how it works", frequency selector, the 3 product
  sections, summary panel, delivery/FAQ, swap modal — is rendered client-side by the
  Preact components already built under
  packages/scripttag/src/subscriptionBoxJoyxjoy/components/. This section
  intentionally contains NO markup for any of that; duplicating it in Liquid would
  just drift out of sync with the real UI.

  Does NOT touch any file already in this theme. Prefix `jw-` only.
{% endcomment %}

{%- comment -%}
  `shop.metafields.avada_custom_landing.data` resolves to the Metafield OBJECT
  (has `.type`/`.value`/...), not the parsed JSON — this metafield has a
  definition (storefront=PUBLIC_READ), and per Shopify's own Liquid docs
  (shopify.dev/docs/api/liquid/objects/metafield, "Accessing metafields of type
  json"), the parsed JSON only lives at `.value`. Reading `cfg.bundles` off the
  Metafield object itself (no `.value`) silently returns nil — no error, just an
  empty loop — which is exactly the bug this line had. `.value` on a nil/missing
  metafield is also nil (Liquid doesn't throw on nil.value), so `cfg` safely ends
  up nil when the metafield hasn't been written yet — the design-mode empty
  notice below (`cfg.bundles == blank`) still fires correctly in that case.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign cfg = shop.metafields.avada_custom_landing.data.value -%}
{%- comment -%}
  App-side plan group to read from each box's `avada_subscription_plan_v2` metafield.
  Blank = emit every group and let the widget decide. Set it to one id (e.g.
  TcK4u92j1Oe8okOKiSl7) to pin exactly which group the page shows.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign plan_group_id = section.settings.plan_group_id | strip -%}

<div class="jw-landing">
  {%- comment -%}
    `custom_font2` is "DM Sans 9pt". theme.liquid interpolates it unquoted into
    --font-body-family, where `9pt` parses as a dimension token and invalidates every
    font-family reading that variable — the text falls back to Times. Re-declare both names
    quoted, scoped to this section rather than fixing the merchant's global layout. The class
    is doubled to outrank the runtime stylesheet, which is appended after this block.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <style>
    .jw-landing.jw-landing {
      {%- if settings.custom_font2 != blank %}
      --jw-font-body: "{{ settings.custom_font2 }}", -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, sans-serif;
      {%- endif %}
      {%- if settings.custom_font1 != blank %}
      --jw-font-heading: "{{ settings.custom_font1 }}", sans-serif;
      {%- endif %}
    }
  </style>
  {%- comment -%}
    ============================================================================
    1) window.AVADA_JW — bundles (spec §4, code block is verbatim from the spec).

    Metafield shape written by updateCustomLandingMetaField
    (packages/functions/src/services/metafieldService.js):
      shop.metafields.avada_custom_landing.data.value == {
        shopId: <string>,
        bundles: [ {productId, handle, name, status}, ... ]
      }
    (`.value` — `shop.metafields.<ns>.<key>` alone is the Metafield object, not
    the parsed JSON; see the `{%- assign cfg = ... -%}` line above.)
    `status` is the merchant's raw form toggle (boolean: true = active, false =
    draft) — see fixedBundleService.js `rebuildCustomLandingMetafield()`, which
    passes `data.status` through unconverted (the ACTIVE/DRAFT string conversion
    only happens for the Shopify productSet call, not for this metafield). Liquid
    truthiness treats only `nil`/`false` as falsy, so `{% if b.status %}` below
    correctly keeps `true` and drops `false` regardless of whether a future write
    ever changes the exact truthy representation.

    Price / image / availability / selling plans are deliberately NOT read from the
    metafield (they would be a stale, wrong-currency snapshot) — they're resolved
    fresh from `all_products[handle]` at render time, same pattern as
    extensions/theme-app-extension/blocks/app-embed.liquid:1-40. A bundle whose
    product handle no longer resolves (deleted/unavailable) is silently skipped
    rather than crashing this section — merchants can delete a Fixed Bundle product
    without this page erroring.
    ============================================================================
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <script>
    window.AVADA_JW = {
      bundles: [
        {%- comment -%}
          Two sources, in priority order.

          1. "Seasonal box collections" — the merchant curates which boxes this page
             offers. No `all_products` lookup at all, so the 20-unique-handle-per-page
             budget never applies. `collection_list` takes up to 20 collections and each
             collection yields up to 50 products, so the ceiling stops being a real one.

          2. `all_products[handle]` over every Fixed Bundle in the shop — what themes
             configured before the picker existed still use. Kept working, but it CANNOT
             show more than 20 boxes: `all_products` resolves 20 unique handles per page
             (shopify.dev/docs/api/liquid/objects/all_products) and silently returns an
             EmptyDrop past that. A box not published to the Online Store channel spends
             one of the 20 and returns nothing. Measured on this merchant: 72 bundles,
             16 resolved, 4 slots burnt on unpublished boxes, 52 never looked at.

          Either way a product is only emitted when the metafield lists it as a live
          Fixed Bundle, so a stray product sitting in a picked collection never renders
          as a box the cart cannot build.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- assign bundle_count = 0 -%}
        {%- assign bundle_handles = ',' -%}
        {%- assign emitted_handles = ',' -%}
        {%- for b in cfg.bundles -%}
          {%- if b.status -%}
            {%- assign bundle_handles = bundle_handles | append: b.handle | append: ',' -%}
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endfor -%}

        {%- if section.settings.box_collections.size > 0 -%}
          {%- for box_collection in section.settings.box_collections -%}
            {%- for p in box_collection.products -%}
              {%- assign needle = ',' | append: p.handle | append: ',' -%}
              {%- comment -%}
                Commas on both sides so `bakery` never matches `bakery-gf`.
                `emitted_handles` guards a product sitting in two picked collections —
                the same de-duplication the category pickers do, for the same reason:
                one entry per product, or the shopper sees the box twice.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              {%- if bundle_handles contains needle -%}
                {%- unless emitted_handles contains needle -%}
                  {%- if bundle_count > 0 -%},{%- endif -%}
                  {%- render 'joy-subscription-bundle-json', product: p, plan_group_id: plan_group_id -%}
                  {%- assign emitted_handles = emitted_handles | append: p.handle | append: ',' -%}
                  {%- assign bundle_count = bundle_count | plus: 1 -%}
                {%- endunless -%}
              {%- endif -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
          {%- endfor -%}
        {%- else -%}
          {%- for b in cfg.bundles -%}
            {%- if b.status -%}
              {%- assign p = all_products[b.handle] -%}
              {%- comment -%}
                Gate on a real property, not on `p` itself: an unresolvable handle yields
                an EmptyDrop, and an EmptyDrop is still truthy in `{% if p %}`. That let
                every unresolvable bundle through with blank title, price, image and
                selling_plan_groups — 72 entries of which only 16 held anything, and no
                trace of why a box never appeared.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              {%- if p.id -%}
                {%- if bundle_count > 0 -%},{%- endif -%}
                {%- render 'joy-subscription-bundle-json', product: p, plan_group_id: plan_group_id -%}
                {%- assign bundle_count = bundle_count | plus: 1 -%}
              {%- endif -%}
            {%- endif -%}
          {%- endfor -%}
        {%- endif -%}
      ],
      planGroupId: {{ plan_group_id | json }},
      {%- comment -%}
        SB-16578 — section 2 only offers staples the merchant put in the plan. The
        catalog endpoint the widget pages through (/collections/*/products.json)
        returns no selling-plan data at all, so plan membership is snapshotted into
        this metafield by buildLandingPlanProducts (rebuilt on every Fixed Bundle
        save). `false` = no snapshot yet → the widget shows everything rather than an
        empty section.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      planProducts: {{ cfg.planProducts | json | default: false }},
      planStatuses: {{ shop.metafields.avada_subscription_plan_v2.statuses | default: false }},
      categories: [
        {%- comment -%}
          ==========================================================================
          2) window.AVADA_JW.categories — section 2/3 catalog (spec §4 "Category
          section 2/3" decision: Shopify Collections, injected via Liquid, merchant
          curates by editing this section's blocks in the theme editor — no code
          change needed to add/remove/reorder a category).

          DEVIATION FROM THE SPEC'S PSEUDOCODE COMMENT — flagged, not guessed past:
          spec §4 shows `categories: [ /* {% for c in collections %} handle, title,
          products */ ]`, i.e. looping the GLOBAL `collections` object (every
          collection in the store). That was left as a comment, not literal code,
          and looping the global object would also surface collections that have
          nothing to do with this page (homepage collections, "frontpage", hidden
          collections, etc.) with no way for the merchant to exclude one without
          deleting the Shopify collection itself. Section **blocks** (`type:
          "category"`, each pointing at one merchant-picked collection) is the
          standard Shopify pattern for "merchant tự đổi danh mục" (spec's own stated
          intent) without touching code, and is what every other section in this
          theme does for collection pickers (see `sections/featured-collection.liquid`
          convention). Confirmed no existing section/template name collides with this
          one in `~/projects/subscriptions/.temp/theme_export__joywholefoods-...zip`.

          Each product is shaped to match exactly what
          ProductPickerSection/pickerLogic.js + ProductCard.js already read from
          `/collections/{handle}/products.js` (the lazy-fetch endpoint) — see
          getVariants()/priceOfVariant()/labelOfVariant() (variant.id/title/price)
          and ProductCard.js (product.id/title/vendor/featured_image/images/variants).
          `price` is cents (Liquid `variant.price`), matching formatMoney.js's
          documented assumption.

          Eager vs lazy — ALL-OR-NOTHING per category, never a partial/truncated
          list. `ProductPickerSection.js:145` only triggers its lazy-fetch when a
          category's `products` array is *empty*
          (`!Array.isArray(target.products) || target.products.length === 0`), and
          `fetchCategoryProducts()` *replaces* `category.products` wholesale
          (`ProductPickerSection.js:123-125`) rather than appending to it. That means
          a truncated (non-empty but incomplete) array would never trigger a fetch,
          and every item past the cut would be permanently unreachable — no
          lazy-fetch, no "load more". So this section emits either the FULL
          collection or an EMPTY array — never a slice.

          THE HARD 50 CEILING (found in round 2 of review, fixed here — read before
          changing `eager_product_limit`): per Shopify's own docs
          (shopify.dev/docs/api/liquid/objects/collection), `collection.products`
          without a `{% paginate %}` tag silently caps at 50 items, while
          `collection.products_count` is the collection's TRUE total. A first version
          of this section gated purely on `c.products_count <= eager_limit` with
          `eager_limit`'s schema `max` at 60 — for a real category with, say, 55
          products and the setting turned up to 55+, `products_count` (55) passed the
          `<= eager_limit` check, but the un-paginated `{% for %}` loop below still
          only emitted Shopify's silent 50-item cap: a non-empty, TRUNCATED array,
          reproducing this exact bug at a different threshold. Fixed two ways
          together (belt + suspenders — either one alone is fragile to a future edit
          of the other):
            1. Schema `max` is now capped at 50 (see settings block) so the setting
               itself cannot request more than Liquid can safely deliver unpaginated.
            2. The Liquid gate ALSO hard-codes `c.products_count <= 50` directly
               (not just `<= eager_limit`) so that even if a future edit raises the
               schema `max` again without touching this file, the loop still refuses
               to run for any collection above 50 and correctly falls back to `[]`
               instead of a silent truncation.
          Ceiling is 50 products, not eager_limit's nominal max — the two happen to
          coincide today because of fix #1, but fix #2 is what actually enforces it.
          A category that comes back empty this way lazy-fetches its full product
          list client-side the first time its tab is clicked (see `handleSetCategory`
          in ProductPickerSection.js) — untouched by this section, just relied upon.
          This is what spec §4's "Danh mục lớn thì lazy-fetch ... khi khách bấm tab"
          is describing structurally.

          TWO SOURCES FEED THIS LOOP (dantt, follow-up to the original spec):
            1. Section settings `staple_collections` / `oneoff_collections`
               (`type: "collection_list"`, schema above) — the RECOMMENDED way to
               add a category now: one picker per step, multi-select, no per-
               collection block needed. Confirmed via Shopify docs
               (shopify.dev/docs/storefronts/themes/architecture/settings/input-settings):
               a `collection_list` setting's Liquid value is an ARRAY OF COLLECTION
               OBJECTS (not handles) — same shape as `collections[handle]` — and
               supports an optional `limit` attribute (default/max 50); this section
               sets `limit: 20` on both (well above the 7 seed collections, still
               under Shopify's 50 hard cap).
            2. Section blocks — `category` (both steps, legacy), `staple_category`
               (step 2 only), `oneoff_category` (step 3 only). Kept ONLY because
               `collection_list` has no per-item field for a tab-label override, and
               this merchant's real collection handles are long
               (`farm-fresh-organics-spray-free-fruit-and-vegetables-delivered-...`)
               — a block is how a merchant renames one specific tab without
               touching code. A collection that doesn't need a custom label never
               needs a block at all now.
          A merchant who wants the SAME collection in both steps picks it in BOTH
          collection_list settings (or adds it as one legacy `category` block).

          De-duplication is by COLLECTION HANDLE across BOTH sources — if the same
          collection is picked via a setting AND a block (or via both step
          settings, or multiple blocks), its `products[]` array is only serialized
          ONCE in the emitted `categories[]` array (never twice), with
          `in_staples`/`in_oneoff` flags on that single entry OR'd across every
          source that referenced it, and the block's `title` override (if any)
          winning over the collection's own title. `LandingApp.js` filters this one
          array into the two per-step lists client-side — see its
          `buildStepCategories` — so the wire payload never doubles up a
          collection's product list just because it's used in both steps.
          ==========================================================================
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          Pass 1 — collect the set of collection handles referenced by either
          settings source or any of the 3 block types, in order, deduped via
          `| uniq`. Liquid has no mutable map/hash, so flags per handle are
          recomputed in pass 2 below by re-scanning both settings arrays (capped at
          `limit: 20` each, schema above) and `section.blocks` (capped at
          `max_blocks: 20`, schema below) for that handle — bounded loops, not
          unbounded.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- assign category_handles_raw = '' -%}
        {%- for c in section.settings.staple_collections -%}
          {%- assign category_handles_raw = category_handles_raw | append: c.handle | append: '|' -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
        {%- for c in section.settings.oneoff_collections -%}
          {%- assign category_handles_raw = category_handles_raw | append: c.handle | append: '|' -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
        {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
          {%- assign block_types = 'category,staple_category,oneoff_category' | split: ',' -%}
          {%- if block_types contains block.type -%}
            {%- assign c = block.settings.collection -%}
            {%- if c -%}
              {%- assign category_handles_raw = category_handles_raw | append: c.handle | append: '|' -%}
            {%- endif -%}
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
        {%- assign category_handles = category_handles_raw | split: '|' | uniq -%}
        {%- assign eager_limit = section.settings.eager_product_limit -%}
        {%- for handle in category_handles -%}
          {%- assign c = collections[handle] -%}
          {%- if c -%}
            {%- comment -%}
              Pass 2 — re-scan both settings arrays (flags only — no per-item
              label) and blocks (flags + title override, first non-blank override
              wins in block order) for this handle. Every source that references a
              handle just ORs its flag(s) in — a block's title override always
              wins over the collection's own title, regardless of which source(s)
              also referenced the same handle.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- assign category_title = c.title -%}
            {%- assign in_staples = false -%}
            {%- assign in_oneoff = false -%}
            {%- for sc in section.settings.staple_collections -%}
              {%- if sc.handle == handle -%}
                {%- assign in_staples = true -%}
              {%- endif -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
            {%- for sc in section.settings.oneoff_collections -%}
              {%- if sc.handle == handle -%}
                {%- assign in_oneoff = true -%}
              {%- endif -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
            {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
              {%- assign block_handle = block.settings.collection.handle -%}
              {%- if block_handle == handle -%}
                {%- if block.settings.title != blank -%}
                  {%- assign category_title = block.settings.title -%}
                {%- endif -%}
                {%- if block.type == 'category' -%}
                  {%- assign in_staples = true -%}
                  {%- assign in_oneoff = true -%}
                {%- elsif block.type == 'staple_category' -%}
                  {%- assign in_staples = true -%}
                {%- elsif block.type == 'oneoff_category' -%}
                  {%- assign in_oneoff = true -%}
                {%- endif -%}
              {%- endif -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
              {%- if forloop.index0 > 0 -%},{%- endif -%}
              {
                "handle": {{ c.handle | json }},
                "title": {{ category_title | json }},
                "in_staples": {{ in_staples | json }},
                "in_oneoff": {{ in_oneoff | json }},
                "products": [
                  {%- comment -%}
                    All-or-nothing: only loop (and therefore only emit any
                    products at all) when the WHOLE collection fits under BOTH the
                    merchant's setting AND Shopify's own hard 50-item cap on an
                    un-paginated `collection.products` (see "THE HARD 50 CEILING"
                    note above — `products_count <= 50` is NOT redundant with
                    `products_count <= eager_limit` even though eager_limit's
                    schema max is also 50 today: this second check is what keeps
                    this loop safe if that schema max is ever changed without
                    updating this file). A collection over either bound gets `[]`
                    here, never a truncated slice.
                  {%- endcomment -%}
                  {%- if eager_limit > 0 and c.products_count <= eager_limit and c.products_count <= 50 -%}
                    {%- for product in c.products -%}
                      {%- if forloop.index0 > 0 -%},{%- endif -%}
                      {
                        "id": {{ product.id | json }},
                        "title": {{ product.title | json }},
                        "vendor": {{ product.vendor | json }},
                        "price": {{ product.price | json }},
                        "featured_image": {{ product.featured_image | json }},
                        "images": {{ product.images | json }},
                        "variants": [
                          {%- for variant in product.variants -%}
                            {%- if forloop.index0 > 0 -%},{%- endif -%}
                            {
                              "id": {{ variant.id | json }},
                              "title": {{ variant.title | json }},
                              "price": {{ variant.price | json }},
                              "compare_at_price": {{ variant.compare_at_price | json }},
                              "available": {{ variant.available | json }},
                              "inventory_quantity": {{ variant.inventory_quantity | json }},
                              "inventory_policy": {{ variant.inventory_policy | json }},
                              "inventory_management": {{ variant.inventory_management | json }}
                            }
                          {%- endfor -%}
                        ]
                      }
                    {%- endfor -%}
                  {%- endif -%}
                ],
                {%- comment -%}
                  Thumbnails for "How it works" steps 2 and 3, emitted separately from
                  `products` above on purpose. That array is all-or-nothing and comes
                  back EMPTY for any collection over the eager ceiling — which is every
                  real category here, since Shopify caps an un-paginated
                  `collection.products` at 50 and these run into the hundreds. Section 2
                  survives that by fetching its own catalog at runtime; the steps section
                  has no fetch, so with no separate field its thumbnails were always
                  blank on a real store.

                  These four must NOT be folded back into `products`: ProductPickerSection
                  treats a non-empty `products` as "already loaded" and skips its
                  lazy-fetch, which would strand every product past the fourth.

                  `limit: 4` also keeps Liquid from fetching the array's default 50
                  products just to read four images.
                {%- endcomment -%}
                "step_images": [
                  {%- for product in c.products limit: 4 -%}
                    {%- if forloop.index0 > 0 -%},{%- endif -%}
                    {{ product.featured_image | json }}
                  {%- endfor -%}
                ]
              }
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
      ]
    };
  </script>

  {%- comment -%}
    ==========================================================================
    Data-empty notice — spec instructs this explicitly ("đừng render root rồi để
    JS chết im lặng"): `avada_custom_landing` is NULL on the dev store until the
    metafield is rebuilt (merchant needs to open+save at least one Fixed Bundle for
    `rebuildCustomLandingMetafield()` to run). A shopper must never see anything
    here; a merchant/dev in the theme editor (`request.design_mode`) must see an
    obvious reason nothing is rendering, instead of a silently blank/broken page.
    ==========================================================================
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if request.design_mode and cfg.bundles == blank -%}
    <div class="jw-landing-empty-notice" style="margin:0 auto;max-width:1226px;padding:24px 20px;">
      <p style="background:#fbecec;color:#9b2c2c;border:1px solid #f0b7b7;border-radius:8px;padding:14px 18px;font:14px/1.5 sans-serif;">
        ⚠️ Joy Wholefoods landing: <code>shop.metafields.avada_custom_landing.data</code> is empty.
        Open and save at least one Product Fixed Bundle in the Joy Subscription admin
        so the metafield gets built — this notice only shows in the theme editor,
        never to customers.
      </p>
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    `all_products` resolves at most 20 unique handles per page
    (shopify.dev/docs/api/liquid/objects/all_products). Past that it returns an
    EmptyDrop for every further handle, with no error, so bundles beyond the 20th are
    simply absent from the page and nothing says why. An unresolvable handle still
    spends one of the 20 — a box that is not published to the Online Store channel
    burns a slot and returns nothing.

    Measured on this merchant: 72 bundles in the metafield, the first 20 handles
    attempted, 16 resolved, 4 spent on unpublished boxes, 52 never looked at.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign bundle_total = cfg.bundles | size -%}
  {%- if request.design_mode and bundle_total > 20 and section.settings.box_collections.size == 0 -%}
    <div class="jw-landing-empty-notice" style="margin:0 auto;max-width:1226px;padding:0 20px 24px;">
      <p style="background:#fff8e1;color:#8a6d1f;border:1px solid #f0dfa6;border-radius:8px;padding:14px 18px;font:14px/1.5 sans-serif;">
        ⚠️ Joy Wholefoods landing: this shop has {{ bundle_total }} Product Fixed Bundles,
        but Liquid can only read <strong>20</strong> of them per page
        (<code>all_products</code> limit). {{ bundle_total | minus: 20 }} bundle(s) are
        invisible to this section, and any box not published to the Online Store sales
        channel still uses up one of the 20 while returning nothing. Pick one or more
        collections under <strong>Seasonal box collections</strong> above — that path
        reads the collections directly and has no such limit. Only shows in the theme
        editor.
      </p>
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if request.design_mode and section.blocks.size == 0 and section.settings.staple_collections.size == 0 and section.settings.oneoff_collections.size == 0 -%}
    <div class="jw-landing-empty-notice" style="margin:0 auto;max-width:1226px;padding:0 20px 24px;">
      <p style="background:#fff8e1;color:#8a6d1f;border:1px solid #f0dfa6;border-radius:8px;padding:14px 18px;font:14px/1.5 sans-serif;">
        ⚠️ Joy Wholefoods landing: no categories configured — sections 2 ("Add your
        staples") and 3 ("This week's one-off items") will show no items. Pick
        collections in "Staple collections (step 2)" / "One-off collections (step
        3)" above, or add a "Category" block for a custom tab label.
      </p>
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}

  <div class="jw-landing-root"></div>
</div>

{%- comment -%}
  ============================================================================
  3) Bundle script — spec §4 point 3.

  URL is a section setting, NOT a hardcoded constant: the CDN URL
  (`cdn-joy-sub.avada.io`) is where CI (`.gitlab/ci/production.yml:199-203`) will
  eventually publish `static/scripttag/*`, but the bundle isn't deployed there yet.
  To test on the dev store today, upload the built `.min.js` to Shopify Files and
  paste that `cdn.shopify.com` URL into this setting — no code change, no redeploy
  of the theme file. Empty setting falls back to the CDN default.
  ============================================================================
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign script_url = section.settings.bundle_script_url | default: 'https://cdn-joy-sub.avada.io/scripttag/avada-subscription-box-joyxjoy-main.min.js' -%}
{%- if script_url != blank -%}
  <script src="{{ script_url }}" defer="defer"></script>
{%- endif -%}

